WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.540
Write a binding reaction in the direction it comes apart: the complex R–L

2
00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:10.060
dissociates into free receptor and free ligand.

3
00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:15.420
The dissociation constant, Kd, is the ratio of loose to bound, and it has units of

4
00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:19.880
concentration. The intuition that makes it usable: Kd is the free-ligand

5
00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:22.780
concentration at which exactly half the receptor is occupied.

6
00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:26.960
The scale runs backwards a smaller Kd means tighter binding.

7
00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:31.920
The classic student error is flipping it: Kd is not Ka; lower is tighter.

8
00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:42.500
Everything so far assumed one site and one ligand, which gives a smooth hyperbola.

9
00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:47.380
The moment a protein has several binding sites that can sense each other, the curve

10
00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:52.720
changes shape. Hemoglobin has four sites, and binding at one changes the affinity of

11
00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:54.540
the others that's cooperativity.

12
00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.200
The result is a sigmoid, an S-shaped curve that, as we'll see, is exactly what a

13
00:01:00.200 --> 00:01:01.580
good oxygen carrier needs.

14
00:01:02.633 --> 00:01:05.053
The Hill coefficient puts a number on the teamwork.

15
00:01:05.833 --> 00:01:10.413
Plot saturation against ligand and fit the Hill equation; its exponent, n, tells you

16
00:01:10.413 --> 00:01:11.813
how much the sites cooperate.

17
00:01:12.573 --> 00:01:16.533
n equals 1 means every site works alone that's myoglobin's hyperbola.

18
00:01:17.233 --> 00:01:19.853
n greater than 1 means positive cooperativity.

19
00:01:20.553 --> 00:01:26.333
Hemoglobin's n is about 2.8, out of a theoretical maximum of 4: strong cooperation,

20
00:01:26.833 --> 00:01:32.173
but not perfect. Treat the Hill equation as a black box that scores how loudly the

21
00:01:32.173 --> 00:01:33.173
subunits talk.

22
00:01:33.933 --> 00:01:37.913
2,3-BPG is the body's dial for oxygen unloading.

23
00:01:38.593 --> 00:01:43.313
It's a small, ferociously charged anion, present in red cells at nearly one-to-one

24
00:01:43.313 --> 00:01:47.713
with hemoglobin, and it wedges into a central cavity between the β chains that is

25
00:01:47.713 --> 00:01:49.733
wide open only in the tense state.

26
00:01:50.333 --> 00:01:55.153
So it acts as a mechanical doorstop, stabilizing T and right-shifting the curve to

27
00:01:55.153 --> 00:01:56.753
dump more oxygen in the tissues.

28
00:01:57.493 --> 00:02:00.853
To reach the relaxed state, that BPG has to be expelled.

29
00:02:01.633 --> 00:02:06.313
Fetal hemoglobin binds it weakly, which is why the fetus runs at higher affinity.

30
00:02:07.433 --> 00:02:09.673
Carbon monoxide is the R-state lock.

31
00:02:10.253 --> 00:02:14.373
It binds heme far more tightly than oxygen and holds hemoglobin in the high-affinity

32
00:02:14.373 --> 00:02:19.453
relaxed state, so the oxygen still aboard holds on more tightly a left-shifted curve

33
00:02:19.453 --> 00:02:21.513
and slashed carrying capacity at once.

34
00:02:22.173 --> 00:02:26.873
The blood turns cherry-red, and a pulse oximeter, which can't tell CO-hemoglobin

35
00:02:26.873 --> 00:02:31.113
from oxygen-hemoglobin, reads falsely normal while the tissues suffocate.

36
00:02:31.793 --> 00:02:35.853
The fix is to flood the system with oxygen and compete the CO back off.
